\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] FPreXXCaseAssign
\item[* relevant examples:]
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) FC\_
PreCaseAssignment
\item[* date of creation:]  7-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch FPreCaseAssignment1-4
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 27-APR-1988\ \ \ \ \ author: Jan Odijk, Margreet Sanders\\
An extra condition is added, so that only models in which no modal occurs are 
refused when they have a case left. For structures with modals, the cases 
of the embedded sentence should remain intact: in generation, they were 
assigned in an earlier cycle, and hence are lawful; in analysis, they will be 
dealt with in a later cycle, after PropSubst has applied. This does not hold 
for the subject of the embedded sentence: in the propsubst, the higher subject 
and the embedded subject are considered identical, including their cases, which 
should therefore both be default. Since in the second cycle (analytically) the 
SubjCaseAssign filter allows [] subjects for infinite clauses, this does not 
cause a problem.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To guarantee the proper application of TXXCaseAssign: all 
structures in which a non-default case occurs are not allowed to go to the Case
-assignment rules (generatively) or to SubjVerbAgreement rules (analytically).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:] 
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] 
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

